.\" ztxplore.1 — manual page for ztxplore, the OpenZFS test lab.
.\" Format: mdoc(7). Render check: mandoc -Tlint docs/ztxplore.1
.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt ZTXPLORE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ztxplore ,
.Nm ztx ,
.Nm kzfs-test
.Nd OpenZFS test lab across six Linux distributions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -tui
.Op Fl -metrics
.Op Fl -results
.Op Fl -runs
.Op Fl -kernel
.Op Fl -results-dir Ar path
.Op Fl -version
.Nm kzfs-test
.Nm kzfs-test
.Cm golden
.Op Ar distro | Cm all
.Op Ar zfs-source
.Nm kzfs-test
.Cm verify
.Op Ar distro | Cm all
.Nm kzfs-test
.Cm run
.Op Fl -quick | Fl -full
.Op Fl -distro Ar list
.Op Ar zfs-source
.Nm kzfs-test
.Cm status
.Nm kzfs-test
.Cm results
.Op Ar run-id
.Nm kzfs-test
.Cm destroy
.Op Fl -all
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs the OpenZFS test suite against six Linux distributions at once, in
virtual machines, on ZFS zvols.
Point it at a pool.
It builds one golden image per distribution, clones them, runs
.Xr zfs-tests.sh 1
inside each clone, and returns a matrix of what passed where.
.Pp
Testing a ZFS change on one distribution proves it works on that
distribution.
The interesting failures are the ones that only appear on a 5.14 EL kernel,
or against a distribution's own ZFS packaging, or on the release where the
DKMS glue changed.
Assembling that harness by hand is a day of work and a pile of VM plumbing.
This is that harness, already built.
.Pp
Two surfaces share one engine:
.Bl -tag -width "kzfs-test"
.It Nm
the native GUI.
In the static terminal-only build this starts the TUI instead.
.It Nm Fl -tui
the terminal UI, for headless hypervisors and SSH sessions.
.It Nm kzfs-test
the harness itself, a shell script with no build step.
Everything the consoles do, they do by calling this.
.El
.Pp
Nothing is hidden.
The consoles shell out to
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr virt-install 1 ,
.Xr zfs 8
and ssh, and every run leaves its full log on disk
.Pq see Sx FILES .
.Sh THE MODEL
A
.Em golden
is a sealed, powered-off VM with ZFS built and the test suite installed.
It is built once and cached.
A
.Em run
clones the goldens and tests the clones, so a run never touches the golden
it came from and a wrecked run is thrown away rather than repaired.
Cloning is a ZFS operation, so a fresh test VM costs a snapshot and a
metadata write rather than a copy of the image.
.Pp
Two rules exist because their absence produced answers that looked fine and
were not:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A golden that comes up without a working ZFS is
.Em refused ,
not sealed.
An image with no ZFS runs zero tests and reports a pass, which is worse than
a failure because nothing tells you.
.It
A run that executes zero tests is scored
.Em error ,
never pass.
The count reaching zero is itself the finding.
.El
.Sh THE CONSOLE
The GUI opens on the lab.
Panes, in the order an operator needs them:
.Bl -tag -width "Metrics"
.It Cm Lab
Build goldens, start runs, watch the live log.
A run started here can be stopped here, and a console reopened mid-run
reattaches to the run already in flight rather than showing an empty screen.
.It Cm Results
The pass/fail/skip matrix per distribution, per run.
.It Cm Runs
Previous runs, newest first.
.It Cm Kernel
The kernel and ZFS version each golden actually carries, which is the pairing
that decides whether a result means anything.
.It Cm Metrics
Links into the Grafana dashboards fed by
.Xr klab-exporter 8
.Pq see Sx METRICS .
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "destroy [--all]"
.It Cm golden Op Ar distro | Cm all
Build golden images.
One-time and cached; a golden that already exists is left alone.
Roughly three to five minutes per distribution when ZFS comes from the
distribution's package, longer when it is compiled from source.
.It Cm verify Op Ar distro | Cm all
Boot each golden, confirm ZFS loads and the test suite is present, power it
back down.
Fast, and the right thing to run before a long suite.
.It Cm run Op Fl -quick | Fl -full
Clone the goldens and run
.Xr zfs-tests.sh 1 .
.Fl -quick
runs a subset, about fifteen minutes across the matrix.
.Fl -full
runs the whole suite, which is hours.
.It Cm status
Goldens, running VMs, last results, pool usage.
.It Cm results Op Ar run-id
Print a run's matrix.
Defaults to the most recent run.
.It Cm destroy Op Fl -all
Destroy the test VMs.
.Fl -all
takes the goldens with them, which means the next run rebuilds from scratch.
.El
.Sh ZFS SOURCES
By default each guest installs the ZFS its own distribution ships, which is
what makes the matrix a statement about distributions.
To test one specific ZFS everywhere instead:
.Bl -tag -width "--zfs-tarball path"
.It Fl -zfs-version Ar x.y.z
Build from an upstream release tarball.
.It Fl -zfs-git Ar owner/repo@ref
Build from a git branch, tag or commit.
.It Fl -zfs-git Ar @ref
Shorthand for
.Ar openzfs/zfs@ref .
.It Fl -zfs-tarball Ar path
Build from a tarball you already have, which is how you test an unpublished
tree.
.El
.Pp
These apply to both
.Cm golden
and
.Cm run .
Applying one to
.Cm golden
bakes that ZFS into the image; applying it to
.Cm run
builds it in the clone.
.Sh THE MATRIX
.Bl -tag -width "CentOS Stream" -compact
.It CentOS Stream
EL kernel lineage
.It Rocky
EL kernel lineage
.It RHEL
EL, and the only one that needs credentials
.It Fedora
current kernels, moves fastest
.It Debian
stable
.It Ubuntu
LTS
.El
.Pp
Each guest is a real VM from the distribution's own cloud image.
Nothing is forked and nothing is patched.
.Pp
RHEL is skipped unless a subscription is available
.Pq see Sx ENVIRONMENT ;
the other five need nothing.
A skipped RHEL is reported as
.Em not built ,
not as a failure.
.Pp
The guest kernel is pinned for the life of the build
.Pq dnf exclude , apt-mark hold , pacman IgnorePkg .
The DKMS module is compiled against the kernel the guest is running, and a
guest that later boots a different one has no module and runs zero tests.
.Sh METRICS
.Xr klab-exporter 8
serves Prometheus metrics on port 9101 while a run is in progress:
pass, fail and skip counts per distribution, run status, the most recent test
seen, and golden readiness with kernel and ZFS labels.
Counts come from each run's summary file when it exists and from a live parse
of the log while it does not, so a run in flight is visible rather than
opaque.
.Pp
Grafana dashboards live under the
.Em ZFS Test Lab
folder: a run matrix, per-VM debug, a live test bench, and the ztxplore
metrics overview.
The exporter is optional.
Without it the tests run exactly the same and only the graphs are missing.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "KZFS_FEDORA_SOURCE"
.It Ev POOL
Pool the goldens and clones live on.
Default
.Pa rpool .
.It Ev ZFS_SOURCE
Where ZFS comes from inside the guest.
Default
.Cm repo .
.It Ev KZFS_FEDORA_SOURCE
Fedora only, default
.Cm git:@master :
Fedora's packaged ZFS routinely lags its own kernel, so tracking git is the
only way that row means anything.
.It Ev RAM
Memory per VM in MB.
Default is what the host has available less 2 GB, because one VM runs at a
time and the shortfall otherwise arrives as the OOM killer taking qemu down
mid-compile.
.It Ev CPUS
vCPUs per VM.
Default half the host, capped at eight.
.It Ev DISK
Disk per VM in GB.
Default 40.
.It Ev SSH_KEY
Key injected into the guests.
Default
.Pa /root/.ssh/id_ed25519 .
.It Ev RHEL_USERNAME , Ev RHEL_PASSWORD
Red Hat subscription credentials, or put them in
.Pa /root/.kzfs-rhel-creds .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/root/kzfs-test/goldens/" -compact
.It Pa /root/kzfs-test/
Results root.
.It Pa /root/kzfs-test/latest
Symlink to the most recent run.
.It Pa /root/kzfs-test/goldens/
Golden build logs, one per distribution, with a
.Pa -latest.log
symlink each.
.It Pa /root/kzfs-test/<run-id>/
Per-run directory:
.Pa <distro>.log ,
.Pa <distro>.status
and, once finished,
.Pa <distro>.summary .
.It Pa /var/lib/libvirt/isos/
Cached cloud images.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A run exits non-zero when any distribution fails or when any distribution
executes zero tests.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Build every golden, then check they work:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kzfs-test golden all
kzfs-test verify
.Ed
.Pp
A quick pass across the whole matrix:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kzfs-test run --quick
.Ed
.Pp
The full suite on two distributions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kzfs-test run --full --distro centos,debian
.Ed
.Pp
Test an unmerged branch everywhere:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kzfs-test golden all --zfs-git @fix-arc-deadlock
kzfs-test run --full
.Ed
.Pp
Test a local tree:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kzfs-test golden debian --zfs-tarball ~/zfs.tar.gz
.Ed
.Pp
Use a pool that is not
.Pa rpool :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
POOL=tank kzfs-test golden all
.Ed
.Pp
Look at the last run, then clean up:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kzfs-test results
kzfs-test destroy
.Ed
.Pp
The console on a headless hypervisor:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ztx --tui
.Ed
